 {"id":"CF-137","title":"P1 - a failed dispatch is never acked, so it redelivers and re-delivers the same failure forever","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"reasoning":null,"acceptance_criteria":"1. Retries are bounded and counted per message id; after the bound the message is acked and recorded as terminally failed with the last output. 2. A deterministic failure (non-zero exit, missing config) is distinguishable from a transient one and does not retry at all. 3. The dispatch row records the attempt count. 4. Evidence: message 085d8834 redelivered at 19:37:51 after failing at 19:08:09, and was left unacked again at 19:41:55.","detail":{"fingerprint":"fcb3782df4ae06e9069b081643e955c2be9f115a0992ca7158737a0fed0d6841"},"created_at":"2026-11-04T00:42:36Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T00:42:36Z"}
 {"id":"CF-138","title":"P1 - the dispatcher kills every real task at 243s while the scheduler budgets 15m40s for the same tick","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"reasoning":null,"acceptance_criteria":"1. The dispatcher derives its wall clock from the dispatch envelope's limits.wall_s, not a module constant. 2. The node-side timeout and the scheduler's await budget are consistent, so a task cannot be killed by the far end while the near end still waits. 3. A test pins the relationship between the two numbers. 4. Evidence: tick code-factory-qa-2026-11-04-00-07-40 awaited 15m40s (closed timeout 19:23:20) while bunker exec killed the same work at 243.4s with exit 124.","detail":{"fingerprint":"80d6a3ba672a94d9a51966b7092182a9ef31628f9b595bb8252622e8302d03a3"},"created_at":"2026-11-04T00:42:36Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T00:42:36Z"}
